  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nAn active afternoon and evening are expected across eastern\nIdaho as an unusually moist and unstable air mass remains\nentrenched across the southeastern portion of the forecast area.\nSurface temperatures climbing into the 80s paired with surface\ndewpoints in the mid-50s are providing sufficient thermodynamic\ninstability for robust convective development. The Storm\nPrediction Center (SPC) maintains a Slight Risk for severe \nthunderstorms across the southeast corner, encompassing the \nSouth Hills, the lower Snake Plain, and the southeastern \nhighlands around Bear Lake. Soundings reveal steep low-to-mid \nlevel lapse rates, creating an environment characterized by \nstrong evaporative cooling within convective cores. Stronger \nthunderstorm cores and organized outflows will be capable of \ngenerating scattered severe wind gusts of 60 to 80 mph within \nthe Slight Risk zone, with a lower but non-negligible wind \nthreat extending across the rest of eastern Idaho. Small hail \nwill also be possible within the strongest cores. High- \nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) show storm activity\npersisting through late evening before tapering off around \nmidnight.\n\nSynoptically, Monday presents a near-identical setup. High- \nresolution model guidance targets the same general footprint \nacross the South Hills, Snake Plain, and southeastern highlands \nfor afternoon shower and thunderstorm coverage. SPC currently \ncarries a Marginal Risk for severe gusts across the area. \nHowever, if daytime destabilization matches today's parameters, \nlocalized severe downburst wind gusts will once again be the \nprimary operational threat.\n\nAs the upper-level trough assisting current convective activity\nexits into Western Canada, high-pressure ridging will attempt \nto rebuild over the Great Basin Tuesday through mid-week. \nResidual low-level moisture trapped beneath the ridge axis will \nmaintain low-end daily shower and thunderstorm chances. \nAfternoon maximum temperatures will run a few degrees above \nseasonal climatological norms, reaching the upper 80s to lower \n90s across lower-elevation valleys.\n\nHeading into the late-week period, a shortwave trough tracking\ninto Washington and British Columbia on Thursday will tighten\nregional pressure gradients, increasing wind speeds across\neastern Idaho on Thursday and Friday. This northern feature will\npartially suppress the Great Basin ridge Friday into the\nweekend, potentially lowering valley temperatures back toward\nseasonal benchmarks in the low-to-mid 80s. However, considerable\nspread remains among model ensemble guidance regarding the exact\nmagnitude of this weekend cooling trend.",
